{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#111","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#111","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#111\",\"parent_revision\":\"f6953539aba7771ae93ecb17aa44c89436223ba1baab4705fb13d89a929a4e71\",\"root_revision\":\"f6953539aba7771ae93ecb17aa44c89436223ba1baab4705fb13d89a929a4e71\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Reproduced\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA `SKILL.md` containing a documented, load-time-substituted link:\\n\\n```markdown\\nSee [the guide](${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/docs/foo.md) for details.\\n```\\n\\nproduces two findings:\\n\\n```\\n\u274c [LK001] internal-links: Broken link: (${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/docs/foo.md) (file not found)\\n\u26a0  [LK002] internal-links: Link missing ./ prefix: (${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/docs/foo.md)\\n```\\n\\n`InternalLinkValidator` treats the literal string as a repo-relative path and calls `Path.exists()` on it, with no expansion step. Reported by another session hitting it in the wild; it blocks `prek run` with no suppression mechanism (no config file, no inline-ignore convention), forcing authors to abandon markdown link syntax entirely.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Upstream Authority This Is Definitely A False Positive\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`code.claude.com/docs/en/skills.md`, cached at `.claude/vendor/sources/claude-code--skills-2026-08-28-0409.md:409`, verbatim:\\n\\n> Claude Code substitutes `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` and `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` in two places: **the skill's markdown content**, and Bash rules in the [`allowed-tools`](#frontmatter-reference) frontmatter. In a plugin skill, Claude Code substitutes `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` and `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}` in the same two places.\\n\\nAnd `.claude/vendor/claude_code/CHANGELOG.md:389`:\\n\\n> Added `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` variable for skills to reference their own directory in SKILL.md content\\n\\nSo substitution in markdown content is documented behaviour, and there are **four** variables, not the two originally reported:\\n\\n| Variable | Scope |\\n|---|---|\\n| `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` | any skill \u2014 the directory containing `SKILL.md` |\\n| `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` | any skill |\\n| `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` | plugin skills only |\\n| `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}` | plugin skills only |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Skilllint Already Does This Inconsistently\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThis is not a missing feature. The tool already expands the same variable elsewhere:\\n\\n- `plugin_validator.py:4439` and `:4792` \u2014 `command.replace(\\\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}\\\", str(plugin_root))`\\n- `plugin_validator.py:4735` \u2014 HK004 lists `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/` as a recognised path prefix\\n\\nSo `HookValidator` substitutes it for hook commands while `InternalLinkValidator` does not for markdown links. Same variable, same tool, two behaviours. The fix has a working precedent in the codebase.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Proposed Fix\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Resolve the four documented variables before the existence check**, reusing the HK004 substitution approach.\\n\\n**For any *other* unexpanded `${...}` token, skip LK001/LK002 entirely.** skilllint has no basis for asserting an unknown variable's expansion is broken \u2014 flagging it would be an invented constraint. Skipping is the honest default.\\n\\nNote HK004 has a related gap worth fixing in the same pass: its prefix list omits `${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}` and `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}`, both documented, so hook scripts referenced that way are silently skipped.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Sequencing\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNot a drive-by fix. LK001/LK002 are mid-migration from `InternalLinkValidator` into `rules/lk_series.py` as callable rule functions (branch `refactor/complete-rule-functions`), under a deliberately behaviour-preserving discipline so the refactor stays verifiable. This fix lands **after** that migration, where the change is a few lines in one rule function with a unit test, rather than surgery on validator internals.\\n\\nAlso queued behind it: LK002 is separately contradicted by upstream \u2014 it warns that relative links need a `./` prefix, but the agentskills spec's own example is `[the reference guide](references/REFERENCE.md)` and Anthropic's skills doc writes `[reference.md](reference.md)`, both bare. LK002 fires on both specs' own worked examples and is a deletion candidate. If LK002 goes, half this issue goes with it.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"98237c0496d0ba5aa8e4aadef5060dc38cc91f1291fa2e8d576aa4b640329cbe\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDORXxKvc8AAAABXP-__g\"}"}